# Flask-SQLAlchemy

This project's ORM is **Flask-SQLAlchemy** — the Flask extension that
wraps SQLAlchemy, not raw SQLAlchemy wired up by hand (that's a
different choice, `orm=sqlalchemy`, covered by the `sqlalchemy` skill).
This skill covers the *integration layer* specific to this extension:
the `db` object, how it gets bound to the app, `db.Model`, and why
queries need an app context. For query syntax, `select()`, joins, and
session semantics that apply to SQLAlchemy generally, see the
`sqlalchemy` skill — this skill doesn't repeat that material.

## When to reach for this

- Touching `src/{{ package_name }}/core/db.py` — the `db`/`migrate`
  objects, `init_db()`, engine options.
- Defining or changing a model in `src/{{ package_name }}/models.py`.
- Debugging `RuntimeError: Working outside of application context` (or
  of request context) coming from a `db.session` call.
{% if migrations %}
- Debugging why `flask db migrate` produces an empty migration, or why
  `flask db upgrade` fails against a fresh database — see
  `references/gotchas.md`, this is a real bug this project's own
  templates hit.
{% endif %}

## Quick reference

- **The extension object**: `src/{{ package_name }}/core/db.py` creates
  `db = SQLAlchemy()` at **module scope** — no `Flask` instance exists
  yet at that point, and none is needed. `db` is a plain importable
  singleton; `from {{ package_name }}.models import Item` (which imports
  `db` transitively) is always safe with no app running.
- **Binding to the app**: `init_db(app, *, testing=False)` calls
  `db.init_app(app)` inside `create_app()` — this is the step that
  actually attaches `db` to *this* `Flask` instance (reads
  `app.config["SQLALCHEMY_DATABASE_URI"]`, sets up the engine/session
  machinery). Never called at import time; see `references/gotchas.md`
  for why `create_app()` itself stays lazy about this. For the
  `create_app()` factory pattern itself, see the `flask` skill.
{% if migrations %}
- **Migrations wiring**: this project has `migrations=true`, so
  `core/db.py` also creates `migrate = Migrate()` and calls
  `migrate.init_app(app, db)` right after `db.init_app(app)` —
  `Migrate` needs the bound `db` object to introspect models against
  the database. This is the only place `flask-migrate` touches this
  skill; the `flask db migrate`/`flask db upgrade` workflow itself is
  the `flask-migrate` skill's territory.
{% endif %}
- **Models**: `src/{{ package_name }}/models.py` — subclass `db.Model`,
  declare columns with `Mapped[...]` type hints and `mapped_column(...)`
  (SQLAlchemy 2.0 style), e.g. `Item`'s
  `id: Mapped[int] = mapped_column(primary_key=True)`. `db.Model`
  (rather than a bare `DeclarativeBase`) is what makes a class usable
  with the rest of the Flask-SQLAlchemy API (`db.session`, the
  `.query` attribute, `Model.__tablename__` defaulting).
- **Queries in a route**: `routes/items.py` uses
  `db.session.execute(db.select(Item)).scalars().all()` for reads,
  `db.session.get(Item, item_id)` for a primary-key lookup, and
  `db.session.add(item)` / `db.session.commit()` / `db.session.delete(item)`
  for writes — `db.select`/`db.session` are Flask-SQLAlchemy's
  re-exports of plain SQLAlchemy, so the query syntax itself is exactly
  what the `sqlalchemy` skill documents. Don't reach for the legacy
  `Model.query` shorthand here; this codebase is consistent about the
  `db.session.execute(db.select(...))` form.
- **Context requirement**: every `db.session` call above runs inside a
  Flask route handler, which always has an active application context.
  Outside a request (a CLI script, a background task, a REPL) you must
  open one yourself — see `references/gotchas.md`.

